content:
We got a template with reaction_id 1679747 that matches our target. It has retro_smarts_template showing product (alkene) and reactants: carbonyl + LiCH2Si(C)(C)C (silyl carbanion). Good.

We need to construct route JSON: root molecule target SMILES, child reaction node with template_id (should be reaction_id? The schema expects "template_id": number? In example they used 1234. So we can use reaction_id 1679747.

Children of reaction are precursor molecules: the carbonyl (p-anisaldehyde) SMILES and the silyl carbanion (p-anisylmethyltrimethylsilane). Need SMILES for those.

p-anisaldehyde SMILES: COc1ccc(cc1)C=O? Actually anisaldehyde (para-methoxybenzaldehyde) SMILES: COc1ccc(C=O)cc1. Yes.

p-anisylmethyltrimethylsilane: anisyl-CH2-SiMe3. SMILES: COc1ccc(cc1)C[Si](C)(C)C? Wait CH2 attached to phenyl: COc1ccc(cc1)C[Si](C)(C)C? The carbon attached to phenyl is CH2 (benzylic). So SMILES: COc1ccc(cc1)C[Si](C)(C)C. However need explicit CH2: COc1ccc(cc1)C[Si](C)(C)C is correct (the carbon attached to phenyl is sp3 carbon with one H? Actually phenyl carbon attached to CH2, so the SMILES is COc1ccc(cc1)C[Si](C)(C)C. That carbon is attached to phenyl and SiMe3, plus one hydrogen (implicit). Good.

Now construct JSON:

{
 "type":"mol",
 "smiles":"C=C(C1=CC=C(OC)C=C1)C2=CC=C(OC)C=C2",
 "children":[
   {
     "type":"reaction",
     "template_id":1679747,
     "children":[
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"COc1ccc(C=O)cc1"},
        {"type":"mol","smiles":"COc1ccc(cc1)C[Si](C)(C)C"}
     ]
   }
 ]
}

Check that template_id is integer. Good.

We should verify that the template matches target using check_smiles_reaction_template_matching.
